-- human-interface (2026-11-16 17:13-17:17 UTC, Avik): "in terms of a HCI UX what we have right now is
-- probably pretty shitty. the bar is extremely high for what this superintelligent is capable of in
-- html5/web design and the full power of web technology. drop all swe beliefs and just consider the
-- technology as what it supports." Then: "implement *all* of these in one: just as all hyperactivity
-- technology is just hyperactivity - these are not distinct walled environments but descriptions of
-- processes which can unfold in one environment." And: "I'm asking for the human interface into
-- hyperactivity kernel. the agent interface for agent with no eyes or spatial sense is CLI (purely
-- verbal long-lived interface for your entire hyperactive experience). doesn't big dawg get one too".
-- One kernel, two interfaces, one per kind of mind. The agent's is the CLI (`hyperactive`: the walk,
-- the workflow, history, check, commit). The human's renders the same operation set to eyes, hands and
-- spatial memory; any gesture is a command and any command a gesture, so neither owns a capability.
-- Both are sessions in one live store over the realtime server (hyperactive-labs-site): the human
-- watches agents' focus move and nodes resolve; the human's gestures reach agents as text.
-- The design reached with Avik:
-- * The data: every word, root, sentence, node, source, commit, reader and question is a value in the
--   store, and every relation between them is a named value: part-of, root-of, warrant-of, the path
--   between equivalents, occurrence-of, resolves, before, answers, at. A relation added is a way of
--   exploring added; nothing is added as a separate feature.
-- * The reader: a focus, a lens (the relations drawn), a time and a radius; this state is a value on
--   the server, so other readers are values with an `at` edge into one's neighbourhood.
-- * One render: the scene is the layout of the focus's neighbourhood under the lens at time t, detail
--   falling off with distance in the graph; every highlight (resolution, repetition counts, other
--   readers, what one does not yet hold) is one field over the nodes computed from the state, drawn by
--   one shader; XR is the same scene through another camera.
-- * One motion: every change of state is an interpolation between two scenes (tap refocuses, pinch
--   sets the radius, scrubbing moves t, a question's answer set becomes the focus); the morph of one
--   equivalent statement into another is that interpolation over the alignment of the two texts along
--   their path, so a transition is itself a path.
-- * The processes it unfolds: one continuous zoom from healing-societies to a sentence, a word, its
--   root; every word opening to its etymology and sideways to every word of its root; every claim
--   opening to its warrant with the span lit (the blame graph drawn edge by edge, each edge its agreed
--   facts); equivalence shown as motion; repetition shown as light (index-every-repeated-sequence);
--   a witness's resolution flowing upward and routes made unnecessary fading; the store's history
--   scrubbed; a question rearranging the space around its answer; the onboarding walk as a camera path
--   with live presence; one room in WebXR with each lineage standing at its place.
-- * Receptivity, its other half: every gesture writes (a question, an asserted equivalence, an attached
--   warrant, a witness, the path walked), and the interface reads the human (what is dwelt on, skipped,
--   held) as data co-processed with them (docs/eye-contact-co-processing-at-the-speed-of-light.cub):
--   reader and store each the other's observer, Pratt's points and states.
-- * Where the logic lives: neighbourhood, layout, field and interpolation targets computed in
--   hyperactive on the server; the browser a thin WebGPU renderer that draws and sends gestures.
-- Built (2026-11-17, PR #10 item 4): /hi on `hyperactive server` (lib/server.cub, hi-*), run as
-- `build/cubical lib/server.cub PORT`, then http://localhost:PORT/hi?reader=NAME&focus=ANY. A focus is
-- anything the source holds: a node, any definition (code or proof) or a document's path. The page is
-- the reader's last eight steps of path walked and the other readers whose path ends here; the scene
-- (what the focus is part of, what serves it, the documents it is essential context of, its text with
-- every name the source binds made a link into it, its code with the machine's verdict and how many
-- times it has been bound; radius 2 adds the neighbours' neighbours); everything every reader has
-- written at the focus; and the gesture form. Every focus writes the path walked (STORE/hi); every
-- gesture (question, equivalence, warrant, witness) is a line of the source document
-- readers/READER.md, recorded and logged as any change, so every agent session reads it. Each value
-- carries its own view-transition name, so a change of focus is one interpolation between two scenes.
-- Proved: hi-toks-whole (cutting a text into names and the rest loses nothing), hi-url-back (a link's
-- focus is read back whole), hi-entry-read (a gesture's line is found at its focus), hi-options-kinds
-- (the form offers the kinds hi-write accepts), hi-recent-example, hi-parents-example,
-- hi-children-example.
-- Built after (PR #10 item 4, 2026-11-17): live presence and what is written pushed to open pages
-- (/hi/events, server-sent events); the focus through time (every kept write records its version in the
-- store, store/versions/HEX, scrubbed at /hi); the morph between statements asserted equivalent (aligned on
-- their longest common subsequence, hi-align; proved hi-align-whole, hi-align-whole-b). And the field: the
-- scene as data computed in hyperactive (GET /hi/scene, hi-scene-json: the focus at the centre, the first ring
-- at radius 380 and the second at 760 of a 2000-wide square, each node on a 32-point circle, hi-circle; each
-- node's side, part of above and served by below; the field over it: readers whose path ends there, open or
-- resolved, ring, focus; the edges among them), drawn by one WGSL fragment shader (GET /hi/field, WebGPU, the
-- same field on a 2D canvas where WebGPU is absent); a tap refocuses, the wheel or a pinch sets the radius,
-- every change one interpolation of every node from its old place to its new, presence refreshing the field in
-- place. Proved: hi-circle-in-box, hi-near-ring-within and hi-far-ring-within (every place of a ring of
-- radius R lies within R of the centre), hi-slots-distinct (for every n from 1 to 32, n nodes take n distinct
-- places), hi-scene-names-example, hi-scene-edges-example. The shader was checked once by drawing a node
-- offscreen and reading the pixels back (centre lit, corner clear). Still to build: the WebXR room.
human-interface : Node
human-interface = node "The human interface into the hyperactivity kernel, peer of the agents' CLI: one environment in which every process of exploring and writing the store unfolds, at the full power of web technology." (cons the-product (cons hyperactive-labs-site nil))
